Chapter: ProbeCtdSubst
  cites Foreword chapter Console

 Does the compiler learn a type variable from a USER-DECLARED parametric
 type the way it learns one from a builtin?

 `subst-type-vars-from-arg` (`IR/LoweringTypes.codex:57-64`) is the walk that
 learns `a := Integer` by matching a parameter type against an argument type.
 It has arms for `TypeVar`, `ListTy`, `FunTy` and `TypeApply`, and an
 `is otherwise -> target` floor. `SumTy`, `RecordTy`, `ConstructedTy`,
 `LinkedListTy`, `VectorTy`, `UnitTy` and `LinearTy` all carry type children
 and all fall to that floor.

 Both cases below are the same shape: a parametric container whose field is a
 closure returning something built from the container's own parameter, with
 the closure's body an `if` whose two branches are CONCRETE. `lower-if`
 (`IR/Lowering.codex:44`) calls `branch-recorded-ty` with those branches as
 the witness, which is exactly the machinery that should replace the variable.

 The ONE RESPECT that differs: case A's payload is a declared sum type and
 case B's is a builtin `List`. If A carries a `tvar` on the wire and B does
 not, the missing arm is the whole cause.

Section: Types

  BoxA (a) = record {
   get : Integer -> StepA a
  }

  StepA (a) = | OneA (a) | DoneA

  BoxB (a) = record {
   get : Integer -> List a
  }

Section: Functions

 A. payload is a DECLARED sum type. Expect `(ctd "StepA" (args (tvar N)))`
 on the wire if the arm is missing.

  mk-a : Integer -> BoxA Integer
  mk-a (n) =
   BoxA { get = \i -> if n == 0 then DoneA else OneA n }

  read-a : BoxA Integer -> Integer
  read-a (b) =
   when ((b.get) 0)
    is OneA (v) -> v
    is DoneA -> 0

 B. NOT A CONTROL, and saying so is the point. It was written as one -- same
 closure, same `if`, a builtin `List` payload that `subst-from-list` DOES
 handle -- and it fails too, which would look like a refutation of A if the
 wire were not read. It is not. Read on the wire, A and B fail at different
 places:

   A  branches `(ctd "StepA" (args int-default))`   -- CONCRETE
      the `if`  `(ctd "StepA" (args (tvar 16)))`    -- the witness was usable
                                                       and the walk learned
                                                       nothing: the missing arm

   B  branches `(list-expr (elems (int-lit 0)) (tvar 19))` -- the BRANCHES
      the `if`  `(list (tvar 19))`                          themselves carry
                                                            the variable, so
                                                            `usable-witness-ty`
                                                            refuses them and
                                                            the substitution is
                                                            never attempted

 So B is a separate defect one layer earlier: a list literal lowered under a
 type-variable expectation does not get a concrete type from its own elements.
 Both branches are non-empty here on purpose -- an empty list literal has an
 unresolved element type for a third reason again, and the first version of
 this probe used one and measured that instead.

 The lesson this probe cost: a control is only a control once its failure
 MODE has been read, not once its verdict has. Two programs can both be red
 for unrelated reasons and the pair says nothing.

  mk-b : Integer -> BoxB Integer
  mk-b (n) =
   BoxB { get = \i -> if n == 0 then [0] else [n] }

  read-b : BoxB Integer -> Integer
  read-b (b) =
   let xs = (b.get) 0
   in if list-length xs == 0 then 0 else list-at xs 0

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing
  opening = act
   print-line-uni (show (read-a (mk-a 7)))
   print-line-uni (show (read-b (mk-b 9)))
  end
